\description{
This function estimates the home range of an animal using the local convex
hull (LoCoH) method of Getz & Wilmers (2004) and Getz et al. (2007). A
convex hull is built around each location and a set of its neighbours; the
hulls are then sorted from the smallest to the largest, and the home range
for a given level is the union of the smallest hulls that, between them,
contain that proportion of the locations. Unlike the minimum convex polygon
(\code{\link[=hr_tt_mcp]{hr_tt_mcp()}}), LoCoH home ranges can have several parts, holes and
concave edges, so they can exclude areas that are never visited.
}
\details{
Events with an empty geometry (i.e. no location) are ignored.

Three ways of choosing the neighbours of each location are available,
depending on \code{type}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"k"} (fixed number of neighbours): each hull is built from a location and
its \code{n} nearest neighbours. Note that \code{adehabitatHR::LoCoH.k()} uses
\code{k - 1} neighbours, so \code{n = k - 1} gives the same hulls.
\item \code{"r"} (fixed radius): each hull is built from all the locations within a
distance \code{n} of a location.
\item \code{"a"} (adaptive): each hull is built from the locations closest to a
location such that the sum of their distances to it is at most \code{n}. Getz
et al. (2007) suggest using the largest distance between any two locations
as a starting value for \code{n}.
}

The neighbour search, the hulls, their areas and the number of hulls needed
for each level are computed in C++ (with a k-d tree for the neighbour
search); the union of the hulls is computed with \code{\link[sf:st_union]{sf::st_union()}}.

The rules follow \code{amt::hr_locoh()}: each
level is matched to the union of hulls whose coverage (the proportion of
locations in the hulls) is closest to it, so the actual coverage can be a
little above or below the requested level. A hull with no area (when all its
locations lie on a line, or are at the same place, as it may be the case
for locations at the den or nest) counts towards the
coverage, but only becomes part of the isopleth if \code{buffer} is positive.
\code{amt::hr_locoh()} buffers every hull by 1e-5 (its \code{rand_buffer}); setting
\code{buffer = 1e-5} here gives the same isopleths.

Distances are computed in the units of the projection of \code{x}, and hulls are
built and merged on a plane. For unprojected data (longitude/latitude),
distances (and so \code{n} for types \code{"r"} and \code{"a"}, and \code{buffer}) are thus in
degrees, and the hulls are distorted (increasingly so away from the
equator); it is recommended to project \code{x} to an appropriate projection
before using this function. Areas are computed with \code{\link[sf:st_area]{sf::st_area()}}, in the
units of the projection of \code{x} (e.g. m^2 for a UTM projection), or in m^2
for unprojected data.
